akes the form of a video game  series that neo now back in a new matrix  as thomas anderson designed the games  play out exactly as our matrix trilogy  does so of course everybody loves them  and anderson is the toast of geekville  this is my very good friend thomas  anderson he is a bonafide famous person  and considered by most to be the  greatest game designer of our generation  importantly situating the trilogy as an  artifact allows people's reactions to  that artifact to be a central part of  the film this creates lots of capacity  for self-examination an intention  telegraphed from the beginning of the  film where we essentially watch two  characters watching the first scene of  the original matrix but of course  there's no story without some conflict  thomas finds himself literally forced by  warner brothers to make a sequel to the  trilogy despite his oath to let sleeping  leather jackets lie i'm sure you can  understand why our beloved parent  company warner brothers has decided to  make a sequel to the trilogy one and  this is not unlike what happened to the  wachowskis irl but we'll get to that  later it's through this storyline that  in our opinion resurrection's most  directly and most successfully examines  the legacy of the matrix in one montage  the dev team bickers about what the  matrix means obviously the matrix is  about transpolitics crypto fascism it's  a metaphor of capitalist exploitation  they debate about what it means to  revive the franchise matrix means mayhem  and they rationalize just what it would  mean to return to the story over and  over again this cannot be another reboot  retread regurgitation why not  reboot cell this is of course boatloads  of fun but it's also a sly reference to  the franchise's reputation for  opaqueness a quality that facilitated so  many disparate interpretations to begin  with but this scene is also key to  contextualizing the developments and  retcons of the entire movie the dev team  we are made to assume wasn't directly  involved with the initial creation of  the matrix games yet each member seems  to have their own definitive  interpretation meanwhile thomas the  driving creative force behind it all  does kung fu in his apartment and takes  luxurious baths so what's going on well  for us it seems like a microcosm of what  notable french smart guy roland barthes  called the death of the author back in  the late 60s barnes was getting sick of  the way literary critics interpreted  everything through the prism of the  author's life in short he claimed that  if a work is sufficiently complex we  can't reliably glean an author's  intentions from the text alone to barths  this means what the author intended  can't be as relevant as what's in the  actual text itself of course this  opinion was misread and over time was  taken to mean that any interpretation of  a text is valid because the only thing  that matters is how the reader feels  which eventually led to say baseless  speculation that breaking bad was all  just a dream after jesse fell asleep in  mr white's chemistry class or you know  the whole red pill ideology the idea of  the death of the author and its  subsequent misreading have all but  defined literary criticism over the  years so by excluding mr anderson the  author of the game from the discussion  resurrections is depicting the dominant  cultural form of criticism and  acknowledging the franchise's capacity  to elicit varying interpretations but  the film is really most concerned with  one interpretation the self-evident  cesspool that is the red pill movement  before you leap into the comments to  tell us our mom should have had an  abortion know that this is not some  spicy hot take the red pill  interpretation flies in the face of  pretty much every other theme in the  trilogy like realizing free will or  resisting oppression what's more the  central villain of resurrections played  with gusto by neil patrick harris  parrots red pill rhetoric with a