ate this  approach introducing auteur theory as an  attempt to elevate the way film was  viewed in academic circles auteur  theorists argued that the director  functions essentially the same way as  the author of a book this arguably risks  understating the contributions of say  their writer the cinematographer  production designer and yeah even the  studio executives so film theorists see  paul sellers proposes collective  authorship as a better fit for movies  where the author is an entity made up of  the people who had an artistic effect on  the final product this can get weird  sometimes though take blade runner the  question of whether protagonist deckard  is a replicant or not is alluded to  throughout the film and it's perhaps the  most prominent area of discussion for  fans director ridley scott has been  super vocal about his take scott says  deckard is a replicant and scenes that  would tacitly confirm this were removed  from the theatrical cut of the movie but  pretty much everyone else involved in  making the film including star harrison  ford and the very dudes who wrote the  script disagree scott got to make his  own cut of the film years later and used  the opportunity to all but confirm his  stance  but then again who does situations like  this draw attention to the fact that  even when altering our notions of  authorship to include collaboration the  question of who should have final say  over a film is always going to be tricky  and it gets trickier when we apply  collective authorship to a franchise  because you get studios eager to  leverage their valuable ip sometimes  over the artistic concerns of the  creators in fact warner brothers was  openly developing a fourth matrix movie  for years even though the wachowskis  were not on board in pretty much all of  the biggest hollywood franchises like  the mcu's bonds and fast and furious the  studio was the only consistent party  across the installments philosopher  theodor adorno explored this calling  hollywood out as a major contributor to  what he called the culture industry now  adorno's thoughts on this are sweeping  and occasionally elitist but of note to  us is his exploration of the hollywood  movie as a product according to adorno  hollywood films are defined and  ultimately diminished by their adherence  to formulas he believed that things like  tropes and strict act structures are  used to make films viable as a product  and nothing that might devalue that  product will be tolerated in the culture  industry any meeting that commodified  art wants to convey is at best  compromised by financial concerns we see  this tension at play in matrix  resurrections too which seems to be  constantly apologizing for its own  existence and going on tangents that  explicitly attack the whole notion of  sequels in the first place i felt either  i'm  having a mental breakdown again  or  i'm living inside a computer-generated  reality that has imprisoned me  again at the same time it plays into  plenty of the obvious ip and profit  driven sequel tropes adorno might have  complained about this makes it all feel  a little messy sure the film pokes fun  at the studio but overall it protects  the franchise still it does raise some  interesting questions as it wrestles  with its own existence fittingly the  analyst who produces the matrix to  generate energy feels almost like an  embodiment of the content industry turns  out in my matrix  the worse we treat you the more we  manipulate you  the more energy you produce  it's nuts  i've been setting productivity records  every year since i took over and the  best part zero resistance people stay in  their pots happier than pigs  this seems to be commenting on the way  this industry and its wealthy gurus  thrive on generating controversy and  stimulating heightened emotions in order  to capture the attention of the masses  more so than taking issue with fan  interpretations then the film saves its  most serious criticism for 